<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Rouen</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Rotomagus</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Rouen is a city on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seine">Seine</a> in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, historic capital of Normandy. As Rotomagus it was the chief town of the Gallic Veliocasses and a Roman river port. Granted with the surrounding land to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vikings">Viking</a> leader <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rollo">Rollo</a> in 911, it became the capital of the Duchy of Normandy and one of the largest towns of medieval <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/joan-of-arc">Joan of Arc</a> was tried and burned there in 1431, and its Gothic cathedral was painted many times by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/claude-monet">Monet</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/rouen.jpg" alt="Rouen" width="320" height="212" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Herbert Frank from Wien (Vienna), AT · CC BY 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Rouen_(37903223574).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Rouen stands on the River Seine in northwestern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> and is the prefecture of the region of Normandy and of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/departement">department</a> of Seine-Maritime. The Seine has been central to its prosperity: in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a> the city had a monopoly on river traffic that reached as far upstream as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a>, and today the Port of Rouen is a major axis for maritime cargo.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Rouen was founded by the Gaulish tribe of the Veliocasses, who called it Ratumacos; the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> called it Rotomagus. It was considered the second city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gallia-lugdunensis">Gallia Lugdunensis</a> after Lugdunum (<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lyon">Lyon</a>), and under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diocletian">Diocletian</a> it was chief city of Lugdunensis secunda, with an amphitheatre and baths. In the 5th century it became the seat of a bishopric and later a capital of Merovingian Neustria.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/normans">Normans</a> overran it from 841, and in the early 10th century it became the capital of the Duchy of Normandy until <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-the-conqueror">William the Conqueror</a> moved his residence to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caen">Caen</a>. It received a charter of self-government in 1150. In the 12th century about 6,000 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a>, a fifth of the population, lived in the town, which had a yeshiva known as La Maison Sublime.</p><p>On 24 June 1204 Philip II Augustus of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> annexed Normandy, and from the 13th century a textile industry based on English wool made the city prosperous. The mayor was assassinated in 1291, Philip IV expelled the Jewish community in 1306, and the Harelle, a revolt of the underclass, in 1389 was suppressed with the loss of the city's charter. Rouen surrendered to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-v-of-england">Henry V of England</a> on 19 January 1419 after a long siege.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/joan-of-arc">Joan of Arc</a> was burned at the stake there on 30 May 1431, and Charles VII recaptured the city in 1449. It withstood a five-month siege by Henry IV and an English force in 1591/2, and in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a> was heavily damaged and its cathedral almost destroyed by Allied bombs.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Rouen is the seat of an archdiocese, with a university and a court of appeal, and its metropolitan area has 715,239 people. Its monuments include Rouen Cathedral, whose Gothic facade <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/claude-monet">Claude Monet</a> painted in a series, the 14th-century astronomical clock known as the Gros Horloge, and the Church of Saint Ouen and the Gothic Church of St Maclou. The Place du Vieux Marché marks the site of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/joan-of-arc">Joan of Arc</a>'s pyre.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Rouen” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Scandinavian raiders had attacked the river valley since the 840s, and by the early tenth century a band led by Rollo had established itself around <b>Rouen</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/duchy-of-normandy">Duchy of Normandy · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>He recovered much of the southern Netherlands for Spain, took Antwerp after a celebrated siege in 1584–1585, and led the Army of Flanders into France to relieve Paris in 1590 and <b>Rouen</b> in 1592; he became duke in 1586 but never returned to rule in person.</blockquote><a href="/empire/duchy-of-parma-and-piacenza">Duchy of Parma and Piacenza · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>In 911 Charles the Simple granted the leader Rollo lands around <b>Rouen</b>, the origin of the Duchy of Normandy.</blockquote><a href="/culture/vikings">Vikings · Contact &amp; Exchange</a></li><li><blockquote>After a six-month siege that left thousands starving, <b>Rouen</b> yielded to Henry V of England, completing his conquest of Normandy.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/january-19">19 January in history</a></li><li><blockquote>The failure led to the Treaty of Saint-Clair-sur-Epte, by which King Charles the Simple granted Rollo lands around <b>Rouen</b>.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/july-20">20 July in history</a></li><li><blockquote>They sold her to the English, who had her tried for heresy and burned at <b>Rouen</b> a year later.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/may-23">23 May in history</a></li></ul></section>
